### Academic Analysis

The naive approach to dynamic range queries utilizes a contiguous array, resulting in a time complexity of $\Theta(1)$ for point updates but $\Theta(N)$ for range sum queries, as it must iterate through up to $N$ elements. Consequently, for $Q$ operations, the worst-case time complexity is $O(QN)$. In contrast, the Segment Tree optimizes this by partitioning the array into a binary tree structure. The construction of the tree follows the recurrence $T(N) = 2T(N/2) + \Theta(1)$, which, by the Master Theorem, yields a total construction time of $\Theta(N)$. Both point updates and range queries follow the recurrence $T(N) = T(N/2) + \Theta(1)$, resulting in a time complexity of $\Theta(\log N)$. Thus, the total complexity for $Q$ operations is $O(Q \log N)$, providing a massive asymptotic improvement over the linear scan.

The height of the Segment Tree is bounded by $O(\log N)$ because the range of each node is halved at every level of the hierarchy. Formally, if the height is $h$, the number of leaf nodes is $N \le 2^h$, which implies $h \ge \log_2 N$. Since the tree is a nearly complete binary tree, the height is strictly $\lceil \log_2 N \rceil + 1$. Regarding space complexity, the Segment Tree requires $\Theta(N)$ space. Specifically, a tree implemented as an array typically requires $4N$ slots to accommodate the nodes of a complete binary tree that can cover $N$ elements, whereas the naive array requires only $N$ slots.

Despite the superior asymptotic efficiency of the Segment Tree, physical hardware constraints introduce critical trade-offs. The naive array benefits from exceptional **spatial locality**; because elements are stored contiguously, they are loaded into the CPU cache in blocks (cache lines), minimizing cache misses during the linear sum. The Segment Tree, however, involves jumping between non-contiguous indices in the `tree` array (e.g., from index $i$ to $2i$ and $2i+1$), which can lead to frequent cache misses and pipeline stalls. Furthermore, the $4\times$ memory overhead of the Segment Tree can be a limiting factor in memory-constrained environments. Therefore, for very small $N$, the constant factors associated with cache locality may make the naive approach faster, but as $N$ grows, the logarithmic growth of the Segment Tree inevitably dominates.
